// case: list-push-into-an-annotated-element-type
// what: the element type an annotation declares is a decision the program made
//      before it ran, so a value that contradicts it is checkable now. It was
//      not checkable at all, and not even at run time: the emitter's element
//      kind comes from a literal or from a push into a list it has already
//      seen, and `val xs: list<int> = []` has neither -- the literal is empty,
//      and the push that follows it is the first thing the emitter is asked to
//      push, so there was no earlier element to contradict. The string went in,
//      nothing compared it with the annotation, and the annotation was the only
//      statement anybody had made about the slot.
//
//        $ orbit check tests/negative/n48_list_push_element_type.orb   (before)
//        Checked ...: no errors.
//        $ orbit run tests/negative/n48_list_push_element_type.orb     (before)
//        (no output, exit 0: the string sat in an int slot unexamined)
//
//      A heterogeneous list is not expressible in this shape of list; see
//      tests/negative/list_mixed_element_kinds.orb for why that is not a gap in
//      the check but a decision about the representation.
// expect-error: This list holds int, so what is pushed into it must be int
fn main() -> int {
    val xs: list<int> = []
    xs.push(1)
    xs.push("two")
    return xs.len()
}